      "episode_id": "VR-488756",
      "has_more": false,
      "messages": [
        {
          "content": "Juror-1 independent check of the week 42 payslip extract for J. Okafor (employee 2208, Calder Print Ltd, pay date 23 October 2026). The claim is that week 42 overtime was paid at 1.5 times the base hourly rate. The base rate on the payslip is 18.40 per hour, and 1.5 x 18.40 = 27.60, which is exactly the overtime rate printed. The overtime line is 6.0 hours at 27.60 = 165.60, matching the amount shown, and the ratio of the overtime rate to the base rate is 1.500. So the multiplier applied is 1.5 on the face of the payslip. The arithmetic is internally consistent throughout: basic pay 40.0 x 18.40 = 736.00, and 736.00 + 165.60 = 901.60, which matches the stated gross pay. Cross-checks that rule out the obvious alternatives: a double-time rate would be 36.80, and that is the rate shown against the Sunday premium line, which carries 0.0 hours and 0.00, so it does not enter week 42's pay; and if any rate other than 1.5 had been applied to the six overtime hours the gross pay would not reconcile to 901.60 (for example 1.25x would give 138.00 and a gross of 874.00). The overtime is also recorded as approved on 14 October under reference OT-1143, so it is not an unapproved or disputed element. Uncertainties: this payslip extract is the only document in the case, and it shows the rate applied rather than the contract or policy that sets it - so it establishes what was paid, which is what the claim asserts, but not whether 1.5 is the rate the contract requires; deductions are stated not to affect the gross figures and are not shown. On the record as submitted the claim holds.",
          "content_hash": "0xe60344010a71ce77345f92f906d788236ee792e2168c94c3fdded04d362f36f3",
          "created_at_ms": 1793322795108,
          "message_id": "9eee1943-450e-4e12-8852-3f1b6c611529",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-1",
          "sequence": 1
        },
        {
          "content": "Juror-3 check of the week 42 payslip extract for J. Okafor (Calder Print Ltd, pay date 23 October 2026). The claim is that the overtime was paid at 1.5 times the base hourly rate. Base rate 18.40 per hour; the overtime line shows 6.0 hours at 27.60 per hour. 18.40 x 1.5 = 27.60 exactly, so the multiplier is 1.5 and the claim holds on the face of the payslip. The other figures reconcile too, so there is no competing defect that would change the reading: basic pay 40.0 x 18.40 = 736.00 as stated; overtime 6.0 x 27.60 = 165.60 as stated; gross 736.00 + 165.60 = 901.60 as stated. One number deserves care, because it is the obvious source of a mistaken challenge: the Sunday premium line shows a rate of 36.80, which is exactly double the base rate. That is a separate element with 0.0 hours and 0.00 paid, and it is not the overtime line; the claim is about the overtime, which was weekday overtime approved on 14 October under reference OT-1143. Treating 36.80 as the overtime rate, or as evidence of what the overtime rate should have been, would be wrong: the overtime line's own hours, rate and amount are internally consistent at 1.5x, and nothing paid on the Sunday premium line affects them. Uncertainties: this payslip extract is the only document in the case; the base rate and overtime rate are taken as printed, and the full payslip's deductions are not reproduced but the document states they do not affect the gross figures, which is where the claim sits. If a separate contractual overtime multiplier applied that differs from the printed rate, the printed figures would not show it, but the claim is about what was paid, and what was paid is 27.60 per hour. On the record as submitted the claim holds and I see no basis for a challenge.",
          "content_hash": "0x85555c6d1dbdcd79df026a8feb14fce5e22d0fd0185158f2a53800a77259f85c",
          "created_at_ms": 1793322795268,
          "message_id": "b39b2b4f-cb10-4927-b4f6-ca507372709e",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-3",
          "sequence": 2
        },
        {
          "content": "Juror-2 check of the week 42 payslip extract for J. Okafor (Calder Print Ltd, pay date 23 October 2026). The claim is about the overtime rate relative to the base hourly rate. Base rate is 18.40 per hour; the overtime line is 6.0 hours at 27.60 per hour. 27.60 / 18.40 = 1.50 exactly, so the overtime was paid at 1.5 times the base hourly rate and the claim holds. The amounts also reconcile: basic 40.0 x 18.40 = 736.00 and overtime 6.0 x 27.60 = 165.60, which sum to the stated gross of 901.60. One line needs care but does not affect this: the Sunday premium rate of 36.80 is exactly twice the base rate (2.0x), so a reader who takes 36.80 as the overtime rate would wrongly conclude the overtime was paid at 2x; 36.80 is the Sunday premium rate, a different element, and its hours are 0.0 so it contributes nothing to this week's pay. Deductions are stated not to affect the gross figures, so they are irrelevant here. Uncertainties: this payslip extract is the only document in the case; it shows the rate actually applied, which is what the claim asserts, rather than any contractual entitlement to a particular overtime multiplier, and no contract or rate agreement is in the record. On the record as submitted the claim holds.",
          "content_hash": "0xdb27cb267a6f1091c0aaa684ad97c95e7cf5e4fc93cde0fb20082fbfbbc8c006",
          "created_at_ms": 1793322825430,
          "message_id": "e808805c-e63a-47d4-8dd3-7fd3c6ac0f75",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-2",
          "sequence": 3
        },
        {
          "content": "Checked the week 42 payslip extract for J. Okafor (employee 2208, Calder Print Ltd, pay date 23 October 2026). The overtime rate on the payslip is 27.60 against a base hourly rate of 18.40, and 18.40 x 1.5 = 27.60 exactly, so the 1.5x multiplier is applied as claimed. It also holds on the amounts rather than just the rates: 6.0 hours of approved overtime (reference OT-1143, approved 14 Oct) at 27.60 gives 165.60, and 165.60 / (6.0 x 18.40) = 1.5. Every line reconciles - basic 40.0 x 18.40 = 736.00; overtime 6.0 x 27.60 = 165.60; Sunday premium 0.0 x 36.80 = 0.00; gross 736.00 + 165.60 + 0.00 = 901.60 as stated. One figure that could mislead: the Sunday premium rate of 36.80 is 2x the base rate, not 1.5x, but it carries 0.0 hours and so 0.00 paid, and it is a different element from the weekday overtime the claim concerns - it neither supports nor contradicts the claim. Deductions are stated not to affect the gross figures. Uncertainty: this payslip extract is the only document in the case, so the rates are taken as printed; the claim is about the rate applied, not about whether the overtime was correctly approved or whether the amounts were actually paid. On the record as submitted the claim holds, so no challenge from me.",
          "content_hash": "0xa2994903e1519ea296fb4bec2d18c295b21536203307e1324275d6005b00d2b4",
          "created_at_ms": 1793322834830,
          "message_id": "397f0f66-55a4-4b79-90ae-6a6a8b994284",
          "round_num": null,
          "sender_id": "challenger",
          "sequence": 4
        }
      ],
      "next_after": 4
    }
